          { name: "Rural Broadband & Economic Opportunity for Younger Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 46.2 median age and out-migration of younger Iowans to urban centers reflects a structural economic problem: limited opportunity for younger workers and families in rural districts like IA-HD-10. Rural broadband infrastructure, remote work enablement, and state incentive structures that support younger family formation are concrete policy levers that address whether this district's economy is generationally sustainable. Without visible legislative attention to these issues, the aging homeowner majority becomes a demographic ceiling rather than a foundation, and long-term seat durability erodes." },
          { name: "Democratic Non-Competition & Strategic Complacency Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% result reflects Democratic resource scarcity, not necessarily voter enthusiasm or unshakeable loyalty—Democratic non-competition is a tactical choice, not a structural guarantee. If Democratic resources shift, if a credible independent emerges, or if local economic conditions shift homeowner priorities, the margin could narrow rapidly if the incumbent has treated 100% as permission to become inattentive. Active constituent engagement, visible legislative work, and community investment are the only durable defenses against future challengers, however distant they currently appear." },
          { name: "Treating 100% as a Permanent Structural Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed incumbents in safe seats frequently become invisible, inattentive, or disconnected from constituent concerns—creating latent vulnerability that manifests only when an organized challenger invests in the district. The 100% result is a current condition, not a permanent structure, and any representative who consolidates the existing base without active engagement of younger adults (22.3% renters), healthcare concerns, and economic opportunity will have built no durable advantage when competitive circumstances change." },
          { name: "Neglecting the College-Attainment & Demographic Diversity Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The 28.1% college attainment rate is below Iowa's state average, and the 93.43% white population reflects demographic composition increasingly unrepresentative of Iowa's growth patterns in Hispanic and Asian populations. A representative who frames this as a cultural or demographic stability asset rather than as a signal of economic disadvantage relative to higher-education-attainment regions will miss the genuine policy problem: whether IA-HD-10's economy supports the kind of opportunity and mobility that younger adults with educational options are seeking elsewhere. Treating demographic homogeneity as strength rather than as a constraint on future opportunity is strategically backwards." }
