          { name: "Working-Family Economic Security & Healthcare Costs", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $64,648 median household income with 19.8% college attainment and a 41.8-year median age, IA-HD-12 is a community of middle-aged, working-class homeowners for whom healthcare costs and property tax management are direct economic pressures. Rural healthcare access, Medicare sustainability, prescription drug affordability, and property tax fairness are the material governance concerns most likely to move voters in this demographic profile. A representative who addresses these issues with specificity and demonstrates a legislative record of delivery on economic security for working families builds the constituent loyalty that makes a 74% margin durable rather than assuming it will hold on partisan grounds alone." },
          { name: "Cost of Living & Property Value Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 77% of the district being white and 77% identifying with one racial category, this is a homeowner-dominant constituency whose economic stake in property values and property tax sustainability is direct and measurable. Inflation, rising housing costs, property tax increases, and the cost of rural service delivery — roads, utilities, emergency services — are the issues that matter to a 41.8-year-old, homeowner-majority district at $64,648 income. A legislative agenda focused on property tax fairness, infrastructure maintenance, and containing the cost of living for homeowners on fixed or modest incomes reflects the constituency's material concerns more accurately than abstract conservative positioning." },
          { name: "Understanding the 26% Opposition & Economic Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "A 74% Republican result in IA-HD-12 is decisive, but the 26% Democratic share represents a floor that any alternative candidate can build from if working-family economic frustration becomes acute. In a district where median income is below state and national averages and college attainment is low, economic anxiety among voters concerned about healthcare costs, job security, and cost of living is a real and present vulnerability. Any incumbent must recognize that the 26% represents not irrelevant voters but genuinely persuadable working people who have not been convinced that current leadership is addressing their economic concerns. Understanding what drives that 26% — whether it is healthcare frustration, property tax anxiety, or simply the belief that government should invest more in rural services — is necessary to contain it and prevent it from growing." },
          { name: "Ideological Consolidation Without Economic Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "IA-HD-12 voters are not voting Republican because they are ideologically committed to conservative doctrine; they are voting Republican because they believe it serves their economic interests as working-class homeowners. Any candidate who campaigns on abstract conservative messaging — opposition to government on principle, ideological purity, partisan tribalism — without addressing the material economic concerns of a $64,648-income constituency is misreading the electorate. Working families have pragmatic concerns, not ideological commitments, and a representative who treats voters as though they are animated by partisan loyalty rather than economic security is vulnerable to a challenger who offers concrete answers to their material needs." },
          { name: "Treating a 74% Margin as Impenetrable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result is dominant, but it is not a guarantee of perpetual electoral safety if the incumbent's record on working-family economic security falters or if an alternative candidate emerges with a credible economic message. In a district where median income is below average, healthcare costs are rising, and property taxes are a direct household concern, voter satisfaction is dependent on visible delivery on these issues. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 74% represents an impenetrable base rather than an alignment that requires continuous constituent engagement and legislative delivery is taking for granted the working-class voters whose economic concerns made them Republican in the first place. A shift of 8-10 percentage points — well within the realm of possibility if economic frustration becomes acute — would narrow this significantly." }
