          { name: "High-Income Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $100,453 median household income and 88% homeownership, IA-HD-22 is a constituency of affluent, asset-holding homeowners whose primary governance concerns center on property tax management, school funding adequacy, and preservation of neighborhood stability. The 59-41 Republican margin reflects this group's baseline alignment, but the 41% opposition in a high-income district signals meaningful dissatisfaction—likely rooted in specific local fiscal or school governance failures rather than ideological disagreement. A Republican candidate holding this seat must demonstrate concrete competence on the tax and education issues that directly affect household budgets at this income level." },
          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Persuadability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 33.2% college attainment in a $100K+ income district, IA-HD-22 contains a significant constituency of educated professionals and dual-income households whose voting behavior increasingly diverges from traditional partisan stereotypes. The 41% Democratic share in a high-income district suggests that college-educated affluent voters in Cedar Rapids suburbs are genuinely persuadable and evaluating on specific governance competence, school quality, and fiscal responsibility rather than partisan inheritance. Any candidate—incumbent or challenger—who can credibly demonstrate competence on education outcomes, property tax fairness, and fiscal transparency will expand their coalition beyond the current 59-41 baseline." },
          { name: "Unknown Demographic Gap & Potential Native American Community", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories (White 93.29%, Hispanic 3.56%, Black 0.45%, Asian 0.32%) account for only 97.62% of the district population, leaving a ~2.4% gap that is likely American Indian/Alaska Native residents. While smaller in proportion than gaps in other Iowa districts, this population deserves specific constituent attention and engagement—particularly if this gap represents members of federally recognized tribes or tribal nations with Iowa presence. Ignoring even a small AIAN constituency in a high-income, educated district where every vote matters in a 59-41 result is a strategic oversight." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as an Insurmountable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result in a high-income, college-educated district with strong Democratic presence is not a comfortable Republican hold—it is a contested suburban seat. The combination of affluent homeowners (a traditionally Republican base) and college-educated professionals (an increasingly persuadable swing constituency) means that a single credible challenger with a message about fiscal competence and school quality could substantially erode the current margin. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 59% represents a durable majority in a Cedar Rapids suburb will underestimate the persuadability of the college-educated 41% opposition." },
          { name: "National Partisan Messaging Over Local Governance Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "IA-HD-22's 41% Democratic share in a high-income district reflects a constituency that is evaluating on local governance performance, school district outcomes, and fiscal responsibility—not national partisan positioning. A candidate who leads with national talking points rather than specific answers about Cedar Rapids property taxes, school funding mechanisms, or local infrastructure will fail to persuade the college-educated, affluent swing voters who determine whether 59% holds or declines. This district rewards granular local competence and punishes abstract partisan rhetoric." }
