          { name: "Workforce & Education Pipeline from East Central Iowa", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.1% college attainment rate in a median-age, median-income suburban district signals a community that values education as the pathway to economic opportunity and community stability. Cedar Rapids is home to major employers and educational institutions; the representative's ability to speak fluently about workforce development, higher education funding, and the pipeline between secondary education and regional employment is direct constituent service. Younger families in this district are evaluating whether their children can build economic lives locally or must migrate elsewhere — a question that connects directly to education investment, employer development, and local economic vitality." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity & Non-White Community Visibility", tag: "careful", why: "At 93.37% white with 2.69% Hispanic, 1.09% Black, and 0.44% Asian, and with the four reported categories totaling 97.49%, this is the most demographically homogeneous district in the dataset — essentially a white-majority, non-Hispanic district in a state and region with increasing Hispanic workforce population. The 72% Republican margin may reflect genuine policy alignment with the homeowner majority, but the near-total absence of visible minority community engagement in a district with 2.69% Hispanic residents risks both representation gaps and the perception that those communities are not considered full constituents. In a tight general election or primary, the credibility differential between a candidate who has invested in authentic relationship with Hispanic and other minority communities versus one who has not becomes material." },
          { name: "Treating 72% as a Durable Majority Rather Than a High-Turnout Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72-28 result in a district this affluent and educated, with strong homeowner alignment and no apparent structural opposition, can create a false sense of security about the seat's durability. The 25.1% college attainment rate signals a constituency that votes on evaluations of candidate competence and policy track record, not inherited partisan loyalty — and suburban swing voters, when mobilized, have repeatedly demonstrated the capacity to shift margins substantially. Any incumbent who treats 72% as a floor rather than as a coalition that requires active maintenance risks being outflanked by a challenger who invests in persuasion and turnout in a district where the electorate's educational profile means they are genuinely evaluable on merit." },
          { name: "Positioning Opposition as Illegitimate or Out-of-District", tag: "avoid", why: "The 28% Democratic result in a district this Republican comes from actual Cedar Rapids and East Central Iowa residents — not from external organizing or demographic change that can be blamed away. Whether concentrated among younger families concerned about education funding, among the small but present minority communities, or among affluent voters evaluating fiscal management on its merits, the 28% represents real constituent concerns that dismissing as 'outside agitation' or ideological extremism alienates the very swing voters most likely to determine competitive outcomes in future cycles. A candidate secure in their record can engage the opposition's actual concerns directly; one who cannot is likely defending against a credible challenge." }
