          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.3% renter rate is unusually high for a district with $98,219 median income, signaling that a substantial portion of the constituency — likely young professionals and dual-income households — is evaluating housing costs as a direct quality-of-life concern. In a competitive 53-47 district, the renter constituency represents a swing group whose voting is not determined by inherited homeowner identity but by whether government is addressing rental affordability, housing supply, and zoning constraints that affect their material living costs. A legislative record or platform that demonstrates concrete action on rental market conditions and housing affordability directly engages the constituency most likely to be persuadable in a narrow margin." },
          { name: "Education Quality & Professional Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 58.4% college attainment and a median age of 35.5, IA-HD-28 is populated by educated professionals whose economic advancement depends on high-quality education for their children and a workforce pipeline that supports professional and entrepreneurial opportunity. Education funding decisions, school quality metrics, and the alignment between K-12 and post-secondary systems are not abstract policy concerns in this district — they are direct determinants of whether young professionals' family formation and career advancement are feasible at the income and education level they have achieved. Any candidate must demonstrate that their record or platform prioritizes education quality as a core economic competitiveness issue." },
          { name: "Understanding the 47% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a high-income, highly educated district, 47% opposition cannot be attributed to demographic diversity or party base enthusiasm alone — it reflects educated professionals and renters actively evaluating the incumbent or party on fiscal and economic outcomes and concluding that an alternative better serves their interests. Whether this opposition is driven primarily by housing affordability frustration, perceived education funding inadequacy, healthcare cost concerns, or fiscal mismanagement is the single most important analytical question for any candidate seeking to hold or win this seat. Identifying specifically what generated the 47% and directly addressing those concerns is essential to consolidating a majority." },
          { name: "Partisan Identity Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 district at $98,219 income and 58.4% college attainment is not motivated primarily by partisan identity or cultural conservatism — it is motivated by material economic concerns, professional competence, and whether government is delivering outcomes. Any candidate who campaigns this seat primarily on abstract partisan themes rather than on concrete fiscal management, housing affordability, education quality, and healthcare access is not engaging the constituency's actual decision-making framework and is leaving 47% of the district unopposed in their own concerns." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Secure Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result in a district composed of young professionals, renters, and educated voters who are evaluating on outcomes is not a secure majority — it is a plurality of contested voters in a constituency that has nearly split its support between two alternatives. Any candidate or incumbent who assumes that 53% represents a durable floor rather than engaging actively with the 47% opposition to understand and address their concerns is making a fundamental strategic error and creating space for a credible challenger to organize around unfilled constituent need." }
