          { name: "Understanding Meyer's Unopposed Status and Latent Opposition", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 100% Democratic result with zero Republican opposition is not a measure of district strength — it is a measure of Republican strategic withdrawal. HD-29's $67,159 median income, 22.8% college attainment, and 32.9% renter rate suggest a working-class to lower-middle-class constituency that should be competitive territory in Iowa politics. The absence of Republican opposition likely reflects resource allocation decisions rather than genuine uncontested Democratic dominance. Any challenger must understand whether Meyer's 100% reflects authentic constituency loyalty or simply the absence of a credible alternative, and build accordingly." },
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Cost of Living", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $67,159 median household income with 32.9% renter rate, HD-29 is a district of households experiencing material cost-of-living pressure — housing affordability, childcare, healthcare, and wage stagnation are the lived economic concerns. The 34.3-year median age signals a constituency managing young families and mortgages at a time when real wages have not kept pace with housing costs. A legislative agenda that directly addresses renter protections, housing affordability programs, childcare accessibility, and wage competitiveness speaks to the actual material conditions of most HD-29 households rather than abstract ideological positioning." },
          { name: "Diverse Urban/Suburban Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "HD-29's demographic diversity — 55.29% white, 21.55% Hispanic/Latino, 11.17% Asian, 9.07% Black, and approximately 2.9% other — requires active, specific engagement across multiple communities rather than generic outreach. The 21.55% Hispanic/Latino population is substantial enough that language access, immigration policy clarity, and workplace protections are not optional considerations but necessary constituent services. The 11.17% Asian population signals a community presence that deserves specific engagement on education policy, small business support, and healthcare access. Treating any of these communities as a secondary demographic concern risks losing active constituent relationships that Meyer may currently hold." },
          { name: "Assuming Meyer's Unopposed Victory Reflects Durable Democratic Control", tag: "avoid", why: "The absence of Republican opposition in 2024 should not be interpreted as evidence of a Democratic floor of 50%+ in this district. Iowa's competitive political environment and the district's working-class economic profile suggest that a well-resourced Republican challenger who speaks credibly to cost-of-living concerns and working-family economic security could generate significant competition. Any Democratic incumbent or challenger who assumes the 100% result indicates a safe seat rather than a district vulnerable to a focused Republican campaign around economic messaging is making a strategic error." },
          { name: "Taking the Diverse Community Coalition for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "Meyer's 100% result depends on holding not just the 55.29% white plurality but also active support from the Hispanic/Latino, Asian, Black, and other minority communities that together constitute 44.71% of the district. These communities did not vote Democratic by default — they voted for Meyer specifically. Any successor who assumes this coalition will automatically transfer without authentic ongoing engagement with each community's specific governance concerns, policy priorities, and constituent service expectations is vulnerable to a challenger who invests in direct community relationship-building." }
