          { name: "Overconfidence in a 100% Result as Stable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% partisan result in any district is politically anomalous and reflects either extraordinary candidate strength or extraordinary opposition weakness — not eternal safety. High-income, college-educated urban districts are capable of rapid political shifts if governance failures accumulate, if economic anxiety among working-class or renter constituents goes unaddressed, or if a credible challenger emerges with a message about neglect or mismanagement. Treating 100% as permission to govern narrowly or to deprioritize constituent service outside the affluent core is how safe seats become vulnerable." },
          { name: "Assuming the Affluent Educated Plurality Remains Reliably Democratic Regardless of Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, high-income urban professionals are capable of voting Republican if they perceive economic mismanagement, tax-policy disadvantage, or that their professional interests are being subordinated to other constituencies. At 54.4% college-educated, this group is the district's numerical plurality, and taking their Democratic loyalty for granted while prioritizing messaging to other constituencies risks alienating the core base. A representative who is visibly accountable to the entire district while defending the educated professional-class interests that generate the Democratic alignment is far more electorally durable than one who assumes affluent professionals will vote Democratic regardless of policy delivery." }
