          "The renter-rate signal at 39.4%—well above the state average—identifies a constituency with direct exposure to housing costs, rental market dynamics, and the policy decisions that shape affordability and stability. Combined with $81,768 income and 47.3% college attainment, this is a district of young professionals, graduate-degree holders, and established middle-income households for whom housing security, education access, and quality-of-life amenities are active political concerns. These voters evaluate on outcomes: are schools funded adequately? Is the housing market delivering affordability or pricing young families out? Are there quality jobs and career pathways that justify staying in-district rather than relocating? The absence of Republican opposition in 2024 does not mean Republican messaging around property tax burden, education choice, or housing deregulation cannot gain traction if a credible candidate articulates it specifically to these voters' lived experience. The Democratic incumbent's task is not to run as though 100% is the baseline—it is to build a genuinely durable coalition by delivering on the material concerns that make upper-middle-class, education-focused, renter-inclusive constituencies stick with a representative across multiple cycles."
