          { name: "Upper-Middle-Class Economic Stability & Tax Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $76,558 median household income and 72% homeownership rate define a prosperous, asset-holding constituency whose economic security depends on stable housing values, property tax fairness, and preservation of middle-class earning pathways. This group typically evaluates government on whether it is protecting their economic position and enabling their children's upward mobility. Democratic performance here reflects alignment on how to structure tax policy and public investment to serve this constituency—not radical redistribution, but fair taxation and public goods that enable middle-class stability. Specific policy focus on property tax reform, education investment that supports college and career pathways, and healthcare cost management directly serves this group's actual concerns." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Constituency Completeness", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 96.72% of the district's population, leaving ~3.3% unaccounted for—a gap that in a district this politically engaged and educated is more likely to represent real constituency groups (multiracial residents, Native American families, recent immigrants, or other populations) than random statistical variation. These populations, however small in percentage terms, deserve specific constituent attention and inclusion in representative engagement. A candidate who appears to assume the district is fully understood through the four major categories risks missing real people whose needs are present even if invisible in aggregate data." },
          { name: "Treating 70-30 as an Immutable District Property Rather Than Incumbent-Dependent", tag: "avoid", why: "The 70-30 margin is almost certainly anchored to Bagniewski's personal standing, name recognition, and constituent relationships built over time, not to an underlying Democratic supermajority in the district itself. If Bagniewski retires or leaves office, a successor who does not invest in building independent constituent relationships and a clear record of policy delivery to the college-educated professional base should not assume that 70-30 will transfer. A 60-40 or even 55-45 result for a successor without Bagniewski's personal brand is a realistic possibility if Democratic coalition-building is neglected during the incumbent's tenure." },
          { name: "Over-Reliance on National Democratic Messaging Rather Than District-Specific Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "IA-HD-35's college-educated professionals are responsive to Democratic messaging on healthcare, education, and climate, but they evaluate primarily on whether their representative is delivering concrete results on the issues that affect their material lives—school funding, healthcare access, property tax management, workforce development. A representative who leads with national partisan messaging rather than a clear, specific legislative record on these district priorities will lose credibility with this sophisticated constituency. The 70-30 margin is sustainable only if the representative can point to genuine policy delivery on the issues that matter to the people who actually live here." }
