          { name: "Younger Renter & Professional Family Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23.2% renter rate in an affluent suburb indicates a constituency of younger professional renters and early-career families whose economic concerns — housing affordability, healthcare costs, childcare, student debt — diverge significantly from homeowner-focused Republican messaging. This is a materially economically secure group ($104k median income) that is not anti-business or anti-market, but is skeptical of rhetoric that ignores their actual cost-of-living constraints. A candidate who addresses young professional economic concerns with policy specificity rather than dismissing them as entitlement will activate a swing constituency in an otherwise Republican-lean district." },
          { name: "Incumbent Positioning Relative to Party Extremism", tag: "careful", why: "Andrews' 52% in a 81% white, 54% college-educated, $104k-income district suggests either the Democrat failed to activate college-educated suburban voters or Andrews has successfully positioned himself as moderate relative to his party on cultural issues and governance. The viability of either strategy hinges on whether suburban voters perceive the incumbent as genuinely responsive to their concerns or as aligned with party extremism on abortion, climate, and education. A challenger who can credibly argue the incumbent has moved rightward on these issues — or an incumbent who has drifted rightward without realizing it — will face significant vulnerability." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Suburban Voters as a Monolithic Base", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban voters are not a unified partisan group; they are a contingent coalition that evaluates candidates on whether their record and rhetoric align with specific material concerns and moderate governance. A candidate who campaigns this seat as though the college-educated majority is a stable Republican base will lose college-educated women and younger professionals to a challenger who speaks directly to healthcare, education, environmental, and abortion-access concerns. The 48% opposition in a district this educated is evidence that the college-educated coalition is actively contested." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Constituency as Electorally Insignificant", tag: "avoid", why: "At 23.2% of the district, renters represent more than one in five voters — a non-trivial swing constituency in a district where the current margin is only 52-48. Younger renters in an affluent suburb are unlikely to be single-issue voters, but they are responsive to candidates who acknowledge their material economic constraints and offer policy responses (housing affordability, student debt, healthcare) rather than dismissing renter concerns as peripheral to homeowner interests. Any campaign that treats renters as an insignificant demographic will lose an active swing group." }
