          "Lead with college-educated suburban quality-of-life governance: schools, environmental protection, healthcare access, and abortion rights are the issues most likely to determine the next election in a 54% college-educated, $104k-income district — and the incumbent's positioning on these issues relative to his party is the vulnerability a challenger will exploit.",
          "Activate the 23.2% renter constituency as a distinct voter group: younger professional renters in a high-income suburb have different economic concerns than homeowners — housing affordability, healthcare costs, and student loan debt are material issues that homeowner-focused Republican messaging may not address, and a candidate who speaks to renter economic concerns can shift the margin.",
          "Do not treat 52% as a stable majority: in a college-educated suburban constituency this affluent, partisan loyalty is contingent on whether the representative's actual record and rhetoric align with voters' material interests and values — a 52% result reflects contested ground, not a consolidated coalition."
