          { name: "Healthcare Cost Management for Aging Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 44.1-year median age and 25% college attainment, IA-HD-60 has a constituency approaching Medicare eligibility and managing healthcare costs for aging parents — and increasingly focused on long-term care, prescription drug pricing, and rural healthcare access. Medicare sustainability, rural hospital viability in surrounding areas, and prescription drug cost policy are direct pocketbook issues for a homeowner majority in this age range with moderate incomes. A legislator who speaks with specificity about protecting Medicare, managing healthcare cost inflation, and supporting rural healthcare infrastructure demonstrates governance competence in the domain that will drive voter evaluation as this cohort continues aging." },
          { name: "The Strategic Risk of Uncontested Incumbency", tag: "careful", why: "Bloomingdale's 100% victory with no Democratic opposition is either a reflection of genuine unanimous support or an artifact of insufficient opposition investment and low incumbent visibility — either way, it is not predictive of future cycles. In a district with this demographic profile, a challenger who offers specific answers to property tax burden, healthcare costs, or housing affordability and builds sufficient visibility to overcome the incumbent's incumbency advantage can rapidly mobilize currently passive voters. The 100% result should trigger heightened constituent engagement and priority clarity, not confidence that the seat is secure absent active management." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology as a Substitute for Material Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "IA-HD-60's demographic profile is structurally Republican-aligned, but this alignment is based on property-ownership and economic moderation, not ideological fervor or partisan identity. A representative who campaigns on abstract conservative positioning or partisan cultural messaging rather than delivering on the material priorities of a $71,204-income homeowner majority — property tax relief, healthcare cost management, housing affordability for adult children — invites a credible challenger to reframe the race around actual constituent concerns. The 100% result should not be interpreted as permission to substitute partisan rhetoric for governance." },
          { name: "Ignoring Non-Homeowner and Renter Constituencies", tag: "avoid", why: "While 79.1% homeowner, IA-HD-60 still has 20.9% renters (~1,500-2,000 households) and the ~1.79% demographic gap (~600-800 residents) whose economic and civic concerns can become invisible in a district-wide narrative focused exclusively on property-owner priorities. Young adult renters struggling with housing affordability, service-sector workers managing cost-of-living pressure, and any demographic minorities in the gap have legitimate constituent needs that exist alongside homeowner priorities. A legislative record that acknowledges only the homeowner majority while dismissing renter and working-class concerns creates the opening for a challenger to mobilize currently underserved constituencies." }
