          "The Republican incumbent's 57% performance is solid but leaves room for Democratic gains, particularly among renters and younger property owners facing affordability pressure. The demographic homogeneity (87.92% white) and small non-white populations (Hispanic 3.87%, Black 3.76%, Asian 0.21%) mean that identity-based messaging—whether progressive or divisive—is neither a winning strategy nor authentic to district concerns. Instead, campaigns must center on pocketbook issues: property tax relief, farm viability, rural infrastructure, and wages relative to cost of living. The 43% Democratic vote share indicates that nearly half the district will listen to economic arguments that prioritize equity and fairness in tax burden and government spending. Candidates who frame their proposals in terms of protecting working families and small operations—not ideology—can shift marginal voters."
