          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2022 victory in a working-class rural district with $65,989 income, 39.7 median age, and 26.6% renter rate is evidence of the absence of opposition in the last cycle, not evidence of durable constituent satisfaction with governance performance on economic security, healthcare access, or housing affordability. Any incumbent who permits this result to generate complacency about constituent engagement, legislative responsiveness, or specific policy accomplishments on rural economic issues will be vulnerable to a challenger who organizes around those concrete concerns." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Rural Hospital Viability as Partisan Litmus Tests", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural healthcare access and rural hospital viability are material governance concerns in IA-SD-3, not partisan cultural issues; a constituency at 39.7 median age with $65,989 income has direct stake in whether rural hospitals remain open and whether healthcare costs for older and working-age adults are manageable. Treating healthcare as a partisan wedge issue rather than as a primary constituent economic concern is a strategic error that cedes this issue to any opponent who frames it as material instead of ideological." }
