          { name: "Working-Class Economic Mobility & Wage Growth", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.6% college attainment rate and $67,862 median income indicate a working-class constituency with constrained economic mobility and wage stagnation. At 26.1% renters—approximately 6,000-8,000 constituent households—there is a meaningful population of working adults in agriculture, manufacturing, service, and trades for whom wage growth, affordable housing, and childcare access are direct economic concerns. Active engagement on workforce development, affordable housing, and living wage policy demonstrates that the incumbent represents all working constituents, not only homeowners, and prevents the emergence of economic-anxiety-based challenges." },
          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access in an Aging District", tag: "careful", why: "With median age 41.1 and 20.6% college attainment in a rural district, IA-SD-4 has an aging population facing acute healthcare access and affordability anxiety as rural hospital closures and physician shortages accelerate across Iowa. Medicare sustainability, rural hospital viability, and mental health service access are constituency concerns that directly affect household economic security and quality of life. The incumbent's attention to these issues—through legislative support for rural healthcare infrastructure, rural physician incentive programs, and Medicaid policy clarity—is the difference between governance that reflects constituent reality and governance that assumes healthcare accessibility is a solved problem." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Victory as Durable Coalition Rather Than Current Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed victory often creates the false confidence that electoral dominance is permanent rather than contextual. A 100% result in a district with 26.1% renters, 5-7% minority population, and significant working-class economic anxiety is likely to reflect current Republican alignment and absence of organized opposition rather than deep-rooted constituent satisfaction. If the incumbent treats the seat as secure and reduces constituent service intensity, active engagement with working-class concerns, and legislative delivery on property tax and rural healthcare, the conditions for a credible challenger to emerge in the next cycle improve substantially." },
          { name: "Overlooking Minority and Renter Community Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The 5.12% Hispanic, 2.27% Black, and estimated ~4% AIAN population, combined with the 26.1% renter rate, represent approximately 8,000-10,000 constituent households whose economic and social concerns—housing affordability, wage growth, healthcare access, and fair treatment—can become invisible when the incumbent focuses exclusively on homeowner priorities. In a district where these communities are politically unorganized and lack strong institutional representation, active neglect can shift sentiment if a challenger offers specific engagement; treating these constituencies as constituent populations with real needs—not as electoral afterthoughts—sustains credibility and prevents organizing opportunities for opposition." }
