          "Engage the renter and working-class minority population with specific economic concern: the 26.1% renter rate and 5-7% Hispanic/Black/AIAN population are not residual—they are 6,000-8,000 constituent households whose wage growth, housing affordability, and healthcare access are real governance concerns that differ from homeowner priorities; active constituent service here sustains credibility if a challenger emerges.",
          "Address rural healthcare access explicitly: the 20.6% college attainment rate and 41.1 median age in a rural district signal an aging population facing direct healthcare access and affordability anxiety; rural hospital viability, Medicaid accessibility, and rural physician recruitment are the governance issues most likely to shift district sentiment if neglected.",
          "Do not treat 100% as a durable coalition: unopposed victory in one cycle often precedes competitive challenge in the next if the incumbent assumes the result reflects stable alignment rather than current advantage; active town halls, constituent service delivery, and explicit engagement of working-class economic concerns prevent the seat from becoming a recruitment target for a challenger."
