          { name: "Latino Community Engagement and Workplace Protections", tag: "lean-into", why: "The Latino population at 19.56% of IA-SD-15 is large enough that specific, ongoing legislative attention to workplace protections, immigration policy clarity, and economic opportunity pathways is a structural requirement for holding the district. Latino workers in urban service and agricultural sectors face distinct economic vulnerabilities — wage theft, unsafe conditions, immigration enforcement uncertainty — that generic Democratic messaging does not address. A legislator who has built authentic relationships with Latino community organizations and brought specific legislative wins on these issues commands credibility; one who treats the community as a voting bloc to be managed rather than a constituency to be served will find that assumption tested by a challenger who makes a credible pitch on economic security." },
          { name: "Understanding the Unopposed 2022 Result as a Data Gap", tag: "careful", why: "A 100-0 result provides no opposition data, no exit polling, and no insight into which parts of the constituency are genuinely consolidated and which are simply uncontested. Without a primary or general election challenger, it is unclear whether the district is at 60% Democratic, 55%, or secured only by superior Democratic turnout organization in a field where Republican or independent candidacy might reveal softer support. Proactive constituent listening, polling, and engagement are essential to understand whether the unopposed victory masks underlying economic anxiety or represents genuine consolidation." },
          { name: "Treating Below-Average Educational Attainment as a Fixed Constituency Characteristic", tag: "avoid", why: "The 21.0% college-educated rate is materially below the state average and suggests a working-class constituency for whom education policy and workforce development are not abstract issues but direct economic concerns. Treating this number as immutable demographic fact rather than as a signal of strong constituent interest in vocational training, apprenticeship access, and pathways to skilled employment misses the opportunity to build legislative leadership on workforce development that addresses the actual educational and economic barriers this population faces." },
          { name: "Assuming Democratic Loyalty Among Young Renters Without Addressing Economic Constraints", tag: "avoid", why: "Young, renting, working-class adults do not automatically remain Democratic out of habit or tribal loyalty — they evaluate on whether they can afford housing, whether wages are adequate, and whether government is actively working to improve their material circumstances. A legislator who campaigns on the premise that IA-SD-15 is safely Democratic because it is urban and diverse, while not delivering specific legislative wins on renter protection, wage increases, or housing affordability, is vulnerable to a credible challenger who promises concrete economic relief. The unopposed 2022 result should not become an excuse to neglect the material concerns that drive voting behavior among a precarious working-age renter constituency." }
