          { name: "Treating 59-41 as a Safe Partisan Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 Republican result in a district with 90.03% white population, 84.7% homeownership, $123,830 median income, and 47.1% college attainment significantly underperforms what the demographic profile alone would predict — it is not a floor but a warning signal. Affluent, educated suburban voters are not locked into partisan loyalty; they evaluate on governance performance. An incumbent who assumes the 59-41 will hold without actively addressing the 41% opposition's concerns about fiscal management, education, and suburban quality of life will find that margin vulnerable to a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity as a Governance Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "At 90.03% white and with very small Hispanic (3.83%), Black (1.92%), and Asian (1.71%) populations, IA-SD-23 is one of Iowa's most homogeneous districts. While this demographic profile is simply the constituency's reality, building a governance strategy around only the majority demographic — or implicitly treating the 7% minority population as peripheral — creates both an ethical governance failure and a latent electoral vulnerability. Any candidate should engage these communities authentically on economic mobility, healthcare access, and representation, not as an afterthought or token acknowledgment." }
