          { name: "Treating 67% as a Permanent Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67% margin in a working-class rural district is not a reflection of overwhelming Republican loyalty but rather the absence of a compelling Democratic alternative that speaks to these households' specific economic concerns. If a challenger emerges with concrete solutions to healthcare costs, property tax burdens, or agricultural economy challenges, persuadable homeowners at $69,990 income will listen. Assuming 67% is durable without active constituent engagement and tangible legislative results is the path to losing a currently safe seat." },
          { name: "Assuming Invisibility Equals Non-Participation", tag: "avoid", why: "The ~5% demographic gap in a rural Iowa district is currently too small to swing an election, but the assumption that emerging communities are politically inert is historically and strategically dangerous. Democratic challengers in rural districts have increasingly focused on immigrant and Hispanic communities as a growth opportunity; passive neglect of these constituencies creates an opening for an organized alternative. Even in a safely Republican district, the strategic cost of explicit marginalization or indifference to emerging communities is higher than the cost of proactive constituent engagement." }
