          { name: "Rural Healthcare and Broadband Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 42.3 median age with a 75.8% homeowner base and moderate income ($68,865), the district's older residents and rural families face direct quality-of-life constraints around rural hospital viability, physician shortages, Medicaid policy, and broadband access. These are not abstract policy questions—they determine whether people can access healthcare without traveling 45 minutes, whether they can work from home or operate a business online, and whether rural communities remain viable or depopulate. A representative who leads on rural infrastructure as a core legislative priority demonstrates that government is investing in the district's future, which is the foundation of durable political support." },
          { name: "Demographic Representation of Hispanic, Asian, and Other Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 96.52% of the population, leaving roughly 3.5% Hispanic/Latino and Asian residents unaccounted for in the aggregate data. While this population is too small to drive the partisan margin, these voters live in the district and deserve constituent service around economic mobility, small-business support, healthcare access, and workforce development. Ignoring them because they do not determine electoral outcome is both a governance failure and a latent vulnerability if future demographic shifts or candidate choice changes and a challenger offers them genuine attention." },
          { name: "Assuming 69% Represents a Mandate to Ignore Opposition Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 margin is genuinely safe, but it represents 31% of voters who chose someone else—and understanding what is driving that 31% opposition is how a representative prevents future challengers from gaining traction. Whether opposition is concentrated among rural voters frustrated by a specific legislative vote, younger adults concerned about economic opportunity, or members of minority communities feeling invisible, treating opposition voices as irrelevant rather than diagnostic risks allowing a credible challenger to build momentum in the next cycle when statewide conditions are more favorable." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Ideologically Homogeneous Rather Than as Voters with Specific Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural Iowa constituencies are often stereotyped as monolithic conservative voters responding only to cultural messaging, but IA-SD-28's actual voters are homeowners with property tax concerns, farmers evaluating commodity policy and water law, families assessing rural hospital viability, and small-business owners deciding whether broadband access makes rural operation feasible. A representative who campaigns on abstract cultural conservatism rather than legislating on the material economic issues that drive household security will lose credibility with the actual voters who make up this district—and will find the 31% opposition growing if an alternative candidate addresses these concerns more directly." }
