          { name: "Healthcare Cost Management for Near-Retiree Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.7 median age combined with 84.7% homeownership creates a demographic cohort approaching retirement with significant healthcare cost concerns: Medicare sustainability, rural hospital viability, prescription drug affordability, and long-term care costs are the practical governance issues for households in this age and asset-holding position. At $85,738 income, many of these households have limited margin for unexpected healthcare expenses, and state-level policy on rural healthcare infrastructure and healthcare workforce development directly affects quality of life. The incumbent's record on these issues is the most likely driver of opposition consolidation or expansion." },
          { name: "Understanding the 34% Opposition in a Demographically Favorable District", tag: "careful", why: "A 66% result in a district with 93.66% white population, 84.7% homeownership, $85,738 median income, and 15.3% renter rate should produce 70%+ Republican outcomes in Iowa's political landscape; the 4-6 point gap between expectation and reality indicates material constituent dissatisfaction that is not explained by demographics alone. Whether driven by property tax frustration, healthcare cost anxiety, rural service decline, or specific local governance failures, identifying what generated the 34% opposition is essential to determining whether the current margin is durable or vulnerable to erosion. Any candidate who assumes the 66% is stable without understanding its opposition is strategically unprepared." },
          { name: "Treating Low College Attainment as a Reason to Rely on Partisan Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "The 25.7% college-attainment rate in IA-SD-33 is below state average, but this does not indicate a constituency that is persuaded by partisan positioning or abstract conservative ideology; rather, it signals a pragmatic, working-and-middle-class population that evaluates candidates on concrete governance outcomes and whether state government is delivering materially for their households. A campaign that relies on generic Republican messaging while failing to address the specific economic concerns of property-owning, healthcare-anxious near-retirees will misread this constituency entirely and risk further opposition consolidation." },
          { name: "Assuming the 66% Margin is a Stable Floor Rather Than a Signal of Vulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "The 66% result in a district with this demographic profile is substantially below the performance expected from Republican candidates in Iowa; a 4-6 point underperformance relative to demographic expectation indicates either a specific local governance failure, a credible challenger capturing swing voters, or constituent dissatisfaction with the incumbent's responsiveness to district concerns. Treating 66% as a comfortable majority rather than a warning signal that one-third of the demographically favorable base is actively voting against the incumbent is a strategic error that will result in further margin erosion or defeat if underlying concerns are not addressed." }
