          { name: "Rural Infrastructure and Property Tax Stewardship", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 79.4% homeownership and $71,175 median income, IA-SD-34 is a community of middle-class homeowners for whom tax burden and public-service delivery are direct household concerns. Road maintenance, county infrastructure investment, and property tax fairness are the governance issues most likely to drive turnout and candidate choice in a district where discretionary income is limited and taxpayers evaluate whether government delivers tangible value. A candidate who demonstrates concrete achievements on rural infrastructure and advocates for property tax reform commands greater credibility than one who addresses these issues generically or abstractly." },
          { name: "Agricultural Economy and Rural Market Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "Iowa's agricultural foundation means that commodity prices, crop insurance, water policy, and market access are material economic concerns for farming and ranching households throughout IA-SD-34. The $71,175 median income reflects a mix of agricultural and non-agricultural employment, but the agricultural constituency is large enough that a candidate's fluency on farm policy directly affects credibility and turnout. A representative who engages agricultural economic development, risk management, and market-access policy with specificity demonstrates attentiveness to the district's economic foundation in a way that generic conservative rhetoric on farming cannot." },
          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access and Hospital Viability", tag: "careful", why: "At 41.9 median age, IA-SD-34's population is aging in place, and rural hospital closures and physician availability are material healthcare concerns for communities whose nearest specialist services may be 45+ minutes away. While the district's strong Republican preference means healthcare policy is not a swing issue, rural voters' frustration with healthcare access is real, and a Democratic challenger could make targeted gains by arguing that the incumbent has not adequately advocated for rural provider support and telehealth infrastructure. Any candidate must demonstrate active engagement with rural healthcare policy — not as an ideological healthcare-reform position, but as a practical rural economic-development issue — to prevent erosion of support among healthcare-conscious rural households." },
          { name: "Treating the 67% Margin as an Unstoppable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67-33 result in Iowa's political landscape is a strong Republican performance, but it is not a supermajority or a guarantee of future stability. At 20.6% college attainment and limited demographic diversity, the district has limited insulation against targeted messaging on rural economic concerns — if a challenger makes a credible case on property taxes, healthcare, or agricultural commodity support, the margin can erode. Any candidate who campaigns defensively, assumes the Republican base will hold without active engagement, or fails to address opposition messaging on rural concerns creates an opening for a Democratic challenger to make material gains." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 3.06% AIAN Gap and Small Minority Populations", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 3.06% American Indian / Alaska Native gap is small, and Hispanic and Black populations are minimal, assuming automatic loyalty from these constituents or failing to deliver specific constituent service weakens margins and signals poor governance practice. At a state legislative level, even small constituency neglect can accumulate into broader disaffection or lower turnout support. A candidate who treats all constituents as worthy of genuine engagement — regardless of whether they appear prominently in the demographic headline — builds a more durable coalition and demonstrates the constituent-service orientation that rural voters value." }
