          { name: "Understanding the 49% Opposition & Persuadable Voters", tag: "lean-into", why: "Nearly half the district chose the Republican alternative in 2024, and in a two-point margin, understanding what drove that 49% and specifically addressing those concerns is the difference between a durable hold and a one-term seat. Whether the opposition stems from homeowner frustration with property tax burden, perceived government inefficiency, economic anxiety, or weakness in the incumbent's profile, identifying the opposition's primary grievance and offering a credible legislative response is the single most important strategic task for consolidating a 51-49 seat into something more stable." },
          { name: "The 5.72% Demographic Gap & Unidentified Constituencies", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 94.28% of the district's population, leaving a 5.72% gap that is almost certainly American Indian / Alaska Native residents, but could include immigrant or other communities whose voting patterns, turnout rates, and constituency preferences are not clear from available data. In a one-point race, even modest shifts in how this 5.72% engages with either candidate are arithmetically meaningful; engaging these constituencies with specificity rather than generic outreach is both a governance obligation and an electoral necessity." },
          { name: "Treating 51% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51-49 result in a district that demographically should lean Republican by a wider margin is not a durable majority—it is a contested plurality where 49% of voters actively prefer the alternative and where any incumbent assuming the seat is safely held is arithmetically wrong. The narrow margin reflects either strong personal candidate performance (which does not transfer to a successor) or structural economic concerns (which persist regardless of who holds the seat), and neither scenario supports confidence in a two-point baseline." },
          { name: "Defaulting to State-Level Cultural Messaging Over Local Economic Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "At 34% college attainment and with 79.4% of residents as homeowners managing property costs and local service delivery, SD-36 is a constituency that evaluates candidates on concrete economic and governance performance more than on state-level cultural or partisan positioning. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns on abstract ideology rather than on what they will specifically do about property taxes, school funding, and local infrastructure will find that messaging misaligned with what the district's 51-49 margin reveals about its actual priorities." }
