        issues: [
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 41.3% college attainment and $81,752 median household income, IA-SD-37 is an educated, affluent suburban district whose voters evaluate candidates on quality-of-life governance: education funding, infrastructure, environmental stewardship, and property tax fairness. The 56% Democratic margin in 2022 reflects this constituency's rejection of partisan polarization in favor of competent, non-ideological governance. Donahue's victory suggests this district rewards pragmatism and constituent service over partisan messaging; any candidate holding or winning this seat must lead on concrete delivery to affluent suburban professionals, not abstract ideology." },
          { name: "Suburban Tax & Service Balance", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 26.1% renters and 73.9% homeowners at $81,752 income, IA-SD-37 is a mixed suburban community where property owners care intensely about tax efficiency and service delivery, while renters evaluate on housing affordability and transportation access. The 39.8-year median age suggests families with school-age children and aging parents — constituencies with direct stakes in education funding, healthcare access, and infrastructure quality. This is not a low-tax, small-government district; it is a quality-of-services district that wants efficient government delivering measurable results on education, roads, and public safety." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity & Inclusion Perception", tag: "careful", why: "At 87.41% white with 3.08% Hispanic, 2.78% Black, and 2.74% Asian, IA-SD-37 is demographically homogeneous, but the district includes meaningful minority communities whose civic participation and sense of representation matter disproportionately in a district where they are small percentages. In a 56-44 Democratic seat where minority turnout and candidate favorability could swing the margin, treating Hispanic, Black, and Asian constituents as visible parts of the community rather than demographic footnotes is both a governance obligation and a strategic asset in a constituency that values inclusive professionalism." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization & Culture War Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "The 56% Democratic margin in a heavily educated, affluent suburban district reflects active rejection of partisan culture-war framing. Candidates who lead on abortion, religious liberty, or identity-politics messaging — whether from the left or right — are swimming against the current of a constituency that wants government focused on tangible delivery, not cultural grievance. This is a district where inflammatory rhetoric on either side is a liability, not an asset." },
          { name: "Treating the 44% Opposition as Irredeemable or Peripheral", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result means the Republican constituency — nearly half the district — includes educated, affluent suburban homeowners with legitimate policy concerns on tax efficiency, government spending, and education curriculum. Any Democratic candidate who consolidates only the existing 56% and dismisses the opposition is leaving the margin vulnerable to a Republican challenger who can credibly speak to the homeowner and fiscal-conservative concerns embedded in that 44%. The path to a durable 56-44 or better requires active engagement with swing voters in that opposition coalition, not assumption that the Democratic base is sufficient." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Central Iowa's Educated Suburban Swing: 56% Democratic in an Affluent, College-Heavy District That Votes on Competence, Not Ideology",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "IA-SD-37 is a metropolitan Des Moines suburban district characterized by education, affluence, and pragmatic evaluation of government performance. The 41.3% college attainment rate and $81,752 median household income place this district substantially above Iowa's state average; the 56% Democratic margin in 2022 reflects an educated suburban electorate that has moved decisively away from partisan conservatism in favor of competent, non-ideological governance focused on concrete delivery. This is not a district where partisan messaging, cultural grievance, or abstract ideology drives voting; Donahue's victory was grounded in constituent service and the perception that Democratic governance delivers measurable results on schools, infrastructure, and property tax management. The 39.8-year median age indicates a constituency of families with school-age children and aging parents — households evaluating schools, healthcare, roads, and public safety on actual performance, not partisan preference. Any candidate holding or winning this seat must lead on tangible delivery: education funding adequacy, infrastructure condition, efficient service delivery, and tax fairness for homeowners and renters alike.",
          "The district's 73.9% homeownership and 26.1% renter rate present a mixed suburban constituency with different but overlapping material concerns. Property-owning families at $81,752 income care intensely about property taxes, school quality, and neighborhood stability; renters care about housing affordability, transportation access, and the economic opportunity that attracts young professionals to the metro area. This is not a low-tax, small-government district — it is a quality-of-services district that wants government spending to be efficient and outcomes measurable. The 87.41% white majority includes meaningful Hispanic (3.08%), Black (2.78%), and Asian (2.74%) communities that, while small in percentage terms, represent real civic constituencies whose visibility and inclusion matter disproportionately in a district where they are minorities. The path to maintaining or expanding the 56% Democratic margin runs through demonstrating that government is delivering for all constituents — schools are improving, roads are maintained, healthcare is accessible, and the community feels inclusive of all its residents. A candidate who focuses only on the Democratic base while dismissing or neglecting the 44% Republican constituency risks vulnerability to a Republican challenger who can credibly speak to homeowner and fiscal-conservative concerns embedded in that opposition coalition."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead on education quality and funding adequacy: with 41.3% college attainment and families with school-age children, this constituency evaluates schools on measurable outcomes and investment level. Any candidate for this seat must articulate a concrete vision for school funding, teacher support, and educational opportunity that reflects the district's professional values.",
          "Address suburban infrastructure and property tax fairness explicitly: the mix of $81,752-income homeowners and renters in a 73.9%-39.8% owner-majority district cares about roads, water systems, and tax policy that balances public investment with efficiency. Voters in this district want government that spends money well, not government that spends less — frame governance as stewardship and accountability, not ideology.",
          "Engage Republican opposition directly on shared suburban concerns: the 44% who voted against Donahue include educated, affluent homeowners with legitimate fiscal-conservative perspectives. Rather than dismissing or marginalizing this constituency, build a persuasive case that the Democratic policy agenda delivers better schools, safer roads, and more efficient government than the alternative — this is where swing voters in the 44% can be moved.",
          "Make minority community visibility and constituent service explicit: Hispanic, Black, and Asian constituents comprise only 8.6% of the district but represent meaningful civic participation and representation expectations. A candidate or incumbent who treats these communities as visible parts of the constituency — not demographic afterthoughts — strengthens both the moral legitimacy and the electoral resilience of the 56% coalition."
        ]
