          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 34.3% renter rate — well above rural Iowa norms — and median household income of $63,977, this is a constituency under real cost-of-living pressure. Renters in this income band face acute affordability constraints on housing, childcare, and healthcare; they are economically present but politically underserved in Iowa's homeowner-dominant political culture. A 65% Democratic result indicates these voters are already predisposed to support candidates who address their material concerns directly. The gap between a strong Democratic margin and the district's modest income level suggests that economic security messaging — specific, concrete, and focused on what renters need — is the lever that consolidates and mobilizes this constituency." },
          { name: "Workforce Development & Educational Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "College attainment at 29.3% — below both state and national averages — signals a working-class and trade-skilled constituency where career pathway messaging has direct resonance. The median age of 35.3 indicates a younger-than-average district where parents are actively evaluating educational options for their children and young adults are assessing their own economic trajectories. In central Iowa's mixed economy of manufacturing, service, and small business, messaging that connects education and workforce development to tangible local job growth — particularly in sectors that pay well without requiring four-year degrees — speaks directly to what this constituency needs and how they evaluate their economic futures." },
          { name: "Democratic Coalition Durability Under Sustained Republican Messaging", tag: "careful", why: "A 65-35 Democratic margin is substantial but not overwhelming in a state that has trended Republican across the same cycle. The district's 75% white population and modest college attainment (29.3%) are demographic groups that have shown responsiveness to Republican economic messaging in recent years, particularly around inflation, housing costs, and business regulation. Maintaining the current 65% coalition requires active engagement with these voters around the specific material concerns driving their Democratic preference — not assuming the margin is durable or that abstract partisan loyalty will hold. Any challenger with credible messaging on cost-of-living relief or economic growth could erode this margin if the incumbent allows the Democratic coalition to become invisible or taken-for-granted." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safely Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65% result in Iowa — a state where Republican performance has strengthened across multiple election cycles — is not a safe Democratic seat; it is a strong margin that reflects active voter preference for the incumbent's positioning on economic issues. Any candidate who campaigns as though this seat is immunized by the current margin, rather than treating every voter conversation as an opportunity to reinforce why renters and working-class families chose Democratic representation, risks losing the active coalition maintenance that produced 65% in the first place. The 34.3% renter rate and modest income level create real economic anxiety that is amenable to Republican messaging if Democrats do not consistently address it." },
          { name: "Allowing Economic Messaging to Become Abstract or Disconnected from Local Reality", tag: "avoid", why: "The constituency's 65% Democratic preference is tied to concrete economic concerns — housing costs, wages, healthcare access — that require specific, locally-rooted policy answers, not national partisan talking points. A candidate who defaults to state or national Democratic messaging without grounding it in what the 34.3% renter constituency and working-class families in central Iowa actually face will find that the margin softens as voters conclude the representation is not attending to their material reality. Generic Democratic positioning in a district this economically focused is a vulnerability, not a strength." }
