          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Material Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $60,327 median income with 38.3% renter rate and 26.2% college attainment, SD-49 is a working-class district where material economic concerns — housing affordability, wage growth, cost of living — drive electoral behavior. The 10.64% Black and 10.19% Hispanic populations, combined with the white working-class majority, represent a constituency whose economic vulnerability transcends racial identity. A legislative agenda grounded in specific solutions to renter affordability, wage floor policy, and access to affordable healthcare is not abstract progressivism — it is the practical governance these voters evaluate on and reward at the ballot." },
          { name: "Demographic Composition & Minority Community Service", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for approximately 91.6% of the district's population, leaving an ~8.4% gap that likely includes American Indian and Alaska Native residents — a constituency invisible in the headline numbers but present and requiring authentic constituent engagement. The 10.64% Black and 10.19% Hispanic communities, combined with this AIAN population, represent meaningful minority representation that cannot be served through generic outreach. Each community has distinct economic and social service needs — criminal justice equity, healthcare access, employment discrimination — that demand specific legislative response rather than assumed alignment with majority-party priorities." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Loyalty Instead of Earning Constituent Trust", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% victory with zero opposition is not a renewable electoral strategy — it is evidence that no credible challenger emerged in the last cycle, not evidence of unshakeable Democratic dominance. The moment a well-funded challenger with authentic working-class economic messaging enters the race, the incumbent's organizational and messaging discipline will determine whether the margin holds or collapses. Any incumbent who interprets 100% opposition as permission to coast rather than a warning signal to deepens constituent relationships and deliver measurable economic progress for renters and working-class households will find that assumption punished." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safely Democratic Rather Than Competitively Holdable", tag: "avoid", why: "SD-49's demographic composition — 26.2% college educated, 38.3% renters, $60,327 income — does not produce Democratic supermajorities automatically; it produces Democratic performance only when Democratic candidates actively serve working-class constituency needs and earn trust through governance. The combination of a relatively low college attainment rate and a substantial renter population means that economic anxiety, housing instability, and wage stagnation are not theoretical concerns but lived daily reality. A candidate who campaigns on inherited partisan loyalty rather than demonstrated commitment to solving these material problems will find that a credible challenger with authentic working-class economic positioning can quickly erode margins in a district this economically vulnerable." }
